.Dd October 27, 2026
.Dt XTC_PREEMPT_INVOLUNTARY 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_preempt_set_involuntary ,
.Nm xtc_preempt_involuntary_supported ,
.Nm xtc_preempt_region_add ,
.Nm XTC_PREEMPTIBLE
.Nd involuntary (signal-context) preemption, EXPERIMENTAL
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc_preempt_involuntary.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_preempt_set_involuntary "int on"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_preempt_involuntary_supported "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_preempt_region_add "const void *start" "const void *end"
.Fd #define XTC_PREEMPTIBLE
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Involuntary preemption lets a timer tick yield a running fiber at the
instruction it interrupted, so its loop-mates run even while it never
reaches a yield point.
The tick source is the per-thread timer of
.Xr xtc_preempt 3
.Pq Fn xtc_preempt_arm , Xr xtc_exec_set_preempt 3 ;
without involuntary preemption a tick only marks the fiber due for its
next cooperative yield.
This header is
.Sy experimental
.Pq docs/abi-stability.md :
its contract may change in any minor release.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_set_involuntary
turns it on
.Pq Fa on No != 0
or off, process-wide.
.Fn xtc_preempt_involuntary_supported
reports whether this build has the redirect: Linux x86-64 (ucontext, or
fcontext with glibc) and Linux aarch64 (either substrate and libc).
.Ss Safe points
A tick yields the fiber only at a
.Em safe point :
outside a critical section
.Pq Fn xtc_proc_critical_enter , Xr xtc_proc 3 ,
outside libxtc's own internal regions, and at a program counter inside a
.Em preemptible range .
The preemptible ranges are:
.Bl -bullet
.It
the main executable's own code, but only when libxtc and libc are both
shared objects (neither lies inside it);
.It
every function marked
.Dv XTC_PREEMPTIBLE
(it is placed in the
.Li xtc_preemptible
section, which is trusted even in a static executable);
.It
every range added with
.Fn xtc_preempt_region_add .
.El
.Pp
Never libc, any other shared library, or libxtc itself.
Code compiled for a thread is not safe to interleave with a loop-mate
on the same thread: glibc's per-thread malloc cache, a
.Vt FILE Ns 's
per-thread recursive lock, any library's thread-local scratch.
Before this check a fiber preempted inside
.Xr malloc 3
let a loop-mate into the same cache (heap corruption within a second),
and loop-mates writing one
.Vt FILE
tore each other's lines.
A tick elsewhere stays pending, and the fiber yields at its next yield
point.
So a fiber that spends a fraction
.Va f
of its time outside preemptible code is sliced only by the ticks that
land in its own code: its slices are about
.Va interval No / (1 \- Va f )
long.
.Ss Static builds must mark their preemptible code
When libxtc
.Pq Pa libxtc.a
or libc is linked statically, its code lies inside the main executable's
text and cannot be told apart from the program's by segment, so the main
executable is not preemptible by default: only
.Dv XTC_PREEMPTIBLE
functions and added ranges are.
With neither, enabling fails with
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
and says why.
The rule is the same on every platform; the only question asked is which
object holds libxtc and which holds libc.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_region_add
adds
.Bq Fa start , Fa end
to the preemptible ranges: code the application vouches for, such as a
JIT buffer or a static build's own objects.
Call it before enabling (a later call lifts a static executable's
refusal); ranges cannot be removed.
.Ss What the interrupted code and the scheduler see
The preempted code resumes with its whole register file, condition
flags, vector state (XSAVE on x86-64; the kernel's signal frame,
including SVE and SME state, on aarch64), x87 state, PKRU and
.Va errno ,
exactly as at the tick.
The scheduler and the fibers it runs in the slice start from a
call-boundary state: on x86-64 DF and AC clear, an empty x87 stack and
clean AVX upper halves; on aarch64 SME streaming mode and ZA off and
TPIDR2_EL0 clear.
MXCSR, the x87 control word and FPCR are per fiber on both substrates.
.Ss Contract for code in a preemptible range
It must not hold per-thread state that a loop-mate on the same thread
could disturb across an instruction boundary (a thread-local buffer
another fiber also uses, a non-recursive lock taken by spinning)
without a critical section.
It may call anything: a tick inside a callee outside the preemptible
ranges is deferred.
.Ss Not supported
CET shadow stacks (the trampoline returns to the interrupted PC with a
plain
.Li ret ) ,
AMX tile state (not saved), and the aarch64 guarded control stack.
macOS, Windows, x86-64 musl and the single-file amalgamation have no
redirect: there a tick always falls back to the cooperative path.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_preempt_set_involuntary
returns
.Dv XTC_OK ;
enabling returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS ,
and leaves involuntary preemption off, where
.Fn xtc_preempt_involuntary_supported
is 0 or no preemptible range can be trusted.
The reason is reported once, at
.Dv XTC_LOG_ERROR
on the default log and on standard error.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_involuntary_supported
returns 1 or 0.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_region_add
returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a NULL
.Fa start ,
an empty range, or one that overlaps libxtc's own text, or
.Dv XTC_E_RESOURCE
when the table (32 ranges) is full.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include "xtc_preempt_involuntary.h"

XTC_PREEMPTIBLE static void
crunch(double *v, size_t n)     /* pure computation */
{
	...
}

if (xtc_preempt_set_involuntary(1) != XTC_OK)
	/* cooperative preemption only: yield in long loops */;
.Ed
